findings.body:
The base size is read with the libc `malloc_usable_size()` call, not the `zmalloc_size()` wrapper that the rest of the code uses. This breaks the default Linux build, which uses jemalloc.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1217-1217"}

Two things go wrong. In `src/zmalloc.h`, the libc `<malloc.h>` include is only pulled in for libc builds. With jemalloc, `malloc_usable_size` is never declared in `object.c`. Modern compilers reject implicit declarations by default, so the build fails. On macOS the same thing happens, because that platform has no `malloc_usable_size`. Even where it compiles, the call goes to glibc's allocator on a pointer that jemalloc allocated (or that zmalloc wrapped with a prefix on other platforms). That is undefined behavior and can return a wrong size.

The PR description says the fix is to use `zmalloc_size((void *)o)`, and the code does not match that. The same macro is used by the old EMBSTR branch and by every other size call in this file.

::callout{type="warning"}
I did not compile the tree in this review. The build-break claim comes from reading `zmalloc.h` and the include graph, not from a build log.

The quicklist branch changed its divisor from `elecount` to `samples`. `elesize` is summed per node, and `elecount` is the number of entries across the sampled nodes. The old code divided by `elecount` to get a per-entry size, then multiplied by the total entry count `ql->count`. The new code divides by the number of sampled nodes, so the result is the per-node average multiplied by the total entry count.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}

For example, a list with 100 entries across 2 nodes, each node costing 10,000 bytes, gives an estimate of 20,000 with the old code, which is correct. The new code gives 10,000 times 100, which is 1,000,000. The estimate is overstated by roughly the number of entries per node.

::callout{type="warning"}
The base-size change above does not touch this line. The regression is purely in the averaging arithmetic.

The stream branch now uses `lpsize * s->rax->numele` instead of `lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1)`. The comment just above says the code should estimate the first N-1 listpacks from the average, then add the real size of the last node. The code below the change adds that last node separately with `asize += zmalloc_size(ri.data)`. With `numele` in the average term, the last listpack is counted twice.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}

The overcount is one listpack per stream with more than `sample_size` listpacks. Its size is roughly the average listpack size, so the error is usually small. It is still a regression in an estimator that is supposed to be accurate, and the code comment contradicts it.

The updated hash and list tests only check that `MEMORY USAGE` is greater than 0. That assertion passes for almost any estimate, including the two regressions above. The new string test checks bounds, but only for strings, so the quicklist and stream math has no coverage that would catch an overcount.

::code{file="tests/unit/type/list.tcl" lines="2241-2246"}

The tests should check the estimate against a known structure. A simple option is to build a multi-node quicklist with a known entry count and entry size, then assert the estimate is within a reasonable factor of entry count times entry size. A stream with more listpacks than `sample_size` would cover the stream branch the same way.
